.TH XTGEOIP 1 "" "xtgeoip 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xtgeoip \- Build and manage xt_geoip data from MaxMind GeoLite2 CSVs.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xtgeoip
[\fB\-b\fR] [\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
.br
.B xtgeoip
\fIcommand\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBxtgeoip\fR is a Rust reimplementation of \fIxt_geoip_build_maxmind\fR
(originally by Jan Engelhardt and Philip Prindeville),
with additional features, correctness improvements, and legacy compatibility mode.
.PP
Public-facing servers are exposed to a constant and large volume of automated
scanning and attack traffic.
Analysis typically shows that a disproportionate share of this traffic
originates from a small number of countries.
GeoIP filtering \(em blocking or logging traffic by country of origin at the
firewall \(em reduces this problem to manageable levels without requiring
ongoing per-address intervention.
.PP
On Linux, GeoIP filtering is provided by the
.B xt_geoip
netfilter kernel module (part of \fIxtables-addons\fR).
Once the binary IP range files are installed and the module is loaded,
a firewall rule can target
.BI "\-\-src\-cc " XX
where
.I XX
is an ISO\ 3166-1 alpha-2 country code or a comma-separated list of them
(e.g.\&
.BR "CN,RU" ).
.PP
\fBxtgeoip\fR handles the full pipeline: downloading the latest GeoLite2 CSV
databases from MaxMind, verifying their integrity, converting them into the
binary format required by
.BR xt_geoip ,
and managing backups, archives, and housekeeping.
Each country produces two output files \(em one for IPv4
.RI ( .iv4 )
and one for IPv6
.RI ( .iv6 )
\(em named by ISO country code (e.g.\&
.IR US.iv4 ,
.IR US.iv6 ,
.IR CN.iv4 ).
.PP
\fBxtgeoip\fR must be run as root to write to the output directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "build " "[\fB\-b\fR] [\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-l\fR]"
Build binary xt_geoip database files from a locally stored GeoLite2 CSV archive.
Optionally back up the current database beforehand
.RB ( \-b ),
clean the output directory first
.RB ( \-c ),
prune old binary tarballs after backup
.RB ( "\-b \-p" ),
or enable legacy output mode
.RB ( \-l ).
.TP
.BI "fetch " "[\fB\-p\fR]"
Download the latest GeoLite2 Country CSV archive from MaxMind,
verify it against the MaxMind-provided SHA-256 checksum,
and store it in the archive directory.
The download is skipped if the current version is already present and verified.
Optionally prune older CSV archives after fetching
.RB ( \-p ).
.TP
.BI "run " "[\fB\-b\fR] [\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-l\fR]"
Run the full pipeline: fetch, then build.
Accepts the same flags as
.B fetch
and
.B build
combined.
When
.B \-p
is used with
.BR run ,
CSV archives (not binary tarballs) are pruned, after the fetch step.
.TP
.BI "conf " "\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-c\fR"
Manage system configuration.
Exactly one of
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-s ,
or
.B \-c
must be given.
.RS
.TP
.B \-d
Show the default (example) configuration with all options documented.
.TP
.B \-e
Open the system configuration file in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.B \-s
Show the current system configuration.
.TP
.B \-c
Prompt for a MaxMind account ID and license key, encrypt them under a
passphrase, and store the result in
.BR /etc/xtgeoip.conf .
Any plaintext credentials already in the file are removed as it is written.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-backup
Back up the current binary database to a compressed tarball in the archive
directory before replacing it.
In verified mode (default), all files named in the BLAKE3 manifest must exist
and pass checksum verification before the backup proceeds.
Use
.B \-f
to override verification and force the backup regardless (useful when the
output directory is in a partially broken state).
Valid with:
.BR build ,
.BR run ,
and top-level invocation.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-clean
Delete the current binary database files from the output directory.
In verified mode (without
.BR \-f ),
only files named in the BLAKE3 manifest are deleted, along with xtgeoip
metadata files (version file, manifest).
With
.BR \-f ,
all
.IR *.iv4
and
.IR *.iv6
files plus xtgeoip metadata are deleted, regardless of the manifest.
When used with
.BR \-b ,
backup always executes before clean.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Override safety checks for
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c .
For backup: proceeds even if the version file, manifest, or some binary files
are missing or fail verification.
For clean: deletes all
.IR *.iv4
and
.IR *.iv6
files regardless of the manifest.
.B \-f
requires exactly one of
.B \-b
or
.B \-c
\(em using
.B \-f
with both in the same invocation is an error (ambiguous intent).
Not valid with
.B fetch
or
.BR conf .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-legacy
Enable legacy output mode.
Reproduces the output of the original Perl implementation, including known
data errors, for compatibility with historically generated files and checksum
matching.
Should not be used for new deployments.
See
.B LEGACY MODE
below.
Valid with:
.B build
and
.B run
only.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prune
Prune older archived versions after the current operation,
retaining the most recent
.I n
versions (configurable; default: 3).
The prune target is determined by context:
.RS
.TP
With
.B fetch
or
.BR run :
prune older GeoLite2 CSV archives from the archive directory.
.TP
With
.B "build \-b"
or top-level
.BR \-b :
prune older binary tarballs from the archive directory.
.PP
Using
.B \-p
without a fetch or backup context
(e.g.\&
.B "build \-p"
without
.BR \-b )
is an error.
Using
.B \-p
in a context where both fetch and backup are active
(e.g.\&
.BR "run \-b \-p" )
is an error: the prune target is ambiguous.
To prune both, run the program twice.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Write the log to
.IR PATH ,
overriding
.I log_file
in the
.B [logging]
section of the configuration.
Because the override is known before the configuration is read, it also
captures a failure to load that configuration \(em which the configured path
cannot, since it is not known until the load has succeeded.
Applies to every command.
Give it after the subcommand
.RB ( "xtgeoip build \-\-log\-file /tmp/x.log" ):
top-level options and subcommands are mutually exclusive, so placing it before
one is rejected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-log
Disable file logging, overriding the
.B [logging]
section.
Terminal output is unaffected \(em only the file sink is conditional, so
messages still reach stdout and stderr.
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-log\-file .
.SH EXECUTION ORDER
The order in which flags are given on the command line is always ignored.
The execution order is fixed and determined by the most logical sequence for
the operations requested.
The invariant is: \fIback up before modifying; acquire before destroying;
clean before building\fR.
.PP
Representative fixed orderings:
.RS
.TP
.B "xtgeoip \-b \-c"
backup \(-> clean
.TP
.B "xtgeoip \-b \-p"
backup \(-> prune binary archives
.TP
.B "xtgeoip build \-b \-c \-p"
backup \(-> prune binary archives \(-> read local archive \(-> clean \(-> build
.TP
.B "xtgeoip run \-c \-p"
fetch \(-> clean \(-> prune CSV archives \(-> build
.RE
.PP
Note that
.B \-\-clean
runs
.I after
the archive is in hand, never before it.
Cleaning first would mean a failed download left
.I output_dir
empty with nothing to install in its place; acquiring the new data first means
a failed run leaves the existing database untouched.
.PP
This means
.B "xtgeoip \-b \-c"
and
.B "xtgeoip \-c \-b"
are identical; the declared order of flags has no effect on the order of
execution.
.SH FILE OWNERSHIP
.B xtgeoip
does not own the output directory; it owns the files its manifest lists.
The manifest is the ownership record, and it defines three categories:
.TP
.B Owned
Listed in the current manifest \(em the
.IR *.iv4 / *.iv6
data files, the
.I version
file, and the manifest itself.
These are backed up, cleaned, and overwritten freely.
.TP
.B Unowned
Everything else in the directory, for example
.BR xtgeoip.conf.example .
These are
.B never
touched, by any operation, including
.BR "build \-c \-f" .
.TP
.B Stale\-owned
Listed in a
.I previous
manifest but not the current one \(em typically
.I EU.iv4
and
.I EU.iv6
after switching out of legacy mode.
These are detected and written to the
.I orphaned
file, then left in place for you to remove.
They are never deleted automatically.
.PP
The unowned guarantee is enforced structurally, not by convention: a file is
eligible for cleaning only if its extension is
.I iv4
or
.I iv6
.B and
its stem is exactly two characters from
.BR [A\-Z0\-9] .
A name such as
.B xtgeoip.conf.example
cannot satisfy that test, so no clean operation can select it.
.PP
Stale manifests
.RI ( *.blake3 ", " *.sha256
from an earlier build) are the one exception to "left for the user": they
are unconditionally superseded by the new manifest and are removed silently.
.SH LEGACY MODE
The original Perl implementation contains a known data error: it maps some
MaxMind continent-code rows to country codes, producing semantically incorrect
output.
.PP
.TP
Continent
.I Asia
(MaxMind geoname_id 6255147) is mapped to country code
.BR AS ,
which is the ISO\ 3166-1 code for American Samoa.
A large number of Asia continent-level IP ranges are therefore incorrectly
merged into the American Samoa country bucket.
.TP
Continent
.I Europe
(MaxMind geoname_id 6255148) is mapped to
.BR EU ,
which is not a valid ISO\ 3166-1 country code.
.PP
Default mode assigns all continent-only and undefined rows to
.B O1
(the IANA-reserved code for unassigned/other), which is semantically correct
and avoids the collision problems above.
.PP
Legacy mode
.RB ( \-l ", " \-\-legacy )
reproduces the original behaviour exactly, including these errors,
for compatibility with historically generated output and for producing
identical checksums to older tooling.
It should not be used for new deployments.
.PP
If you previously ran \fBxtgeoip\fR with \fB\-\-legacy\fR and later switch to
default mode, two orphaned files will remain in the output directory:
.IR EU.iv4
and
.IR EU.iv6 .
These are listed in the
.I orphaned
file in the output directory and are safe to remove.
.PP
Which clean form removes them depends on
.B when
you act, because
.B \-\-clean
runs before
.B build
regenerates the manifest:
.TP
.B xtgeoip build \-c
Use this
.I during
the switch back \(em in the same invocation that leaves legacy mode. The
manifest on disk still lists
.BR EU ,
so the files are still owned and a plain clean removes them.
No
.B \-\-force
is required.
.TP
.B xtgeoip build \-c \-f
Use this
.I after the fact
\(em once a default-mode build has already rewritten the manifest. The
manifest no longer lists
.BR EU ,
so the files are stale-owned and
.B \-\-force
is needed to remove them.
This is the form the orphan warning advises, because by the time it prints
you are already in this case.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The system configuration file is
.B /etc/xtgeoip.conf
(TOML format).
An example configuration with all options documented is provided at
.BR /usr/share/xt_geoip/xtgeoip.conf.example ;
copy it to
.B /etc/xtgeoip.conf
and edit as required.
.PP
Every section rejects keys it does not define, and an unrecognised key is a
parse error rather than a warning.
A key that is silently ignored cannot be told apart from one that works, so
a misspelling would otherwise leave the default in force while appearing to
have been set.
.PP
To download GeoLite2 databases, a free MaxMind account is required.
Obtain one at
.IR https://www.maxmind.com/en/geolite2/signup ,
then create a license key from the account dashboard.
.PP
Credentials are never written to the configuration file in plaintext.
Run
.B "xtgeoip conf \-c"
and supply the account ID, the license key, and a passphrase.
The pair is encrypted and stored as
.B [maxmind.credentials]
in
.BR /etc/xtgeoip.conf ;
any plaintext
.B account_id
or
.B license_key
already present in the file is removed as the ciphertext is written.
.PP
The passphrase is requested again on every operation that contacts MaxMind
.RB ( fetch
and
.BR run ).
It is stored nowhere, so those commands cannot run unattended.
.B build
rebuilds from the cached archive and needs no passphrase.
.PP
Additional configuration sections:
.RS
.TP
.B [paths]
.I archive_dir
(default:
.IR /var/lib/xt_geoip ),
.I output_dir
(default:
.IR /usr/share/xt_geoip ),
and
.I archive_prune
\(em how many archived versions to keep (default: 3).
.TP
.B [maxmind]
.I url
\(em the GeoLite2 CSV download endpoint.
It must use
.IR https :
the license key is sent as HTTP basic auth on the first request, so a
cleartext origin would expose it before any redirect is involved.
The
.I credentials
sub-table is written by
.B "xtgeoip conf \-c"
and should not be edited by hand.
No other key is accepted in this section, as above.
.TP
.B [logging]
.I log_file
\(em path to the log file
(default:
.IR /var/log/xtgeoip.log ).
.TP
.B [processing]
.I threads
\(em number of worker threads used to parse the CSVs and build the database.
Absent, or set to
.BR 0 ,
means use every available core.
.RE
.PP
Use
.B "xtgeoip conf \-s"
to display the active configuration, or
.B "xtgeoip conf \-d"
to display the full default configuration with comments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B xtgeoip -h
display usage
.TP
.B xtgeoip -b
backup database
.TP
.B xtgeoip -c
clean database
.TP
.B xtgeoip -b -c
backup then clean
.TP
.B xtgeoip -b -p
backup then prune
.TP
.B xtgeoip -b -f
force backup database
.TP
.B xtgeoip -b -c -p
backup then prune then clean
.TP
.B xtgeoip build
build database
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -l
build (legacy mode)
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -b -p
backup then prune then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -c -f
clean then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -b -c -p
backup, prune, clean, build
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -b
backup then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -c
clean then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -b -c
backup then clean then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip build -b -f
force backup then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip conf -s
show configuration
.TP
.B xtgeoip conf -d
show default configuration
.TP
.B xtgeoip conf -e
edit configuration
.TP
.B xtgeoip conf -c
prompt for and encrypt MaxMind credentials
.TP
.B xtgeoip fetch
fetch CSVs
.TP
.B xtgeoip fetch -p
fetch then prune archives
.TP
.B xtgeoip run
fetch then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -l
fetch then build (legacy)
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -p
fetch then prune then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -c -p
fetch then clean then prune then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -c -f
fetch then force clean then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -b
backup then fetch then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -b -c
backup then fetch then clean then build
.TP
.B xtgeoip run -b -f
force backup then fetch then build
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xtgeoip.conf
System configuration file.
.TP
.I /usr/share/xt_geoip/xtgeoip.conf.example
Example configuration with all options documented.
.TP
.I /var/lib/xt_geoip/
Archive directory.
Stores downloaded GeoLite2 CSV archives and binary database tarballs (backups).
.TP
.I /usr/share/xt_geoip/
Output directory.
Stores binary IP range files
.RI ( *.iv4 ,
.IR *.iv6 ),
the version file, the BLAKE3 manifest, and (when present) the orphaned file
list.
